{"reference":"Hebrews 13","book":"Hebrews","chapter":13,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Hebrews closes by gathering its instruction into embodied communal practices: sustaining sibling love, welcoming strangers, remembering prisoners and mistreated people, honoring marriage, resisting the love of money, receiving teaching, offering praise, doing good, and sharing. These commands belong together rather than serving as isolated tests of social standing, sexuality, or prosperity.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 13:1-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_013_96cc8cca2ea17cc65e3d4513"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Closing Instructions","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The calls to remember and obey leaders are framed by their teaching, the example of their lives, and their accountability. This framing does not authorize authoritarian control or require silence about complaints. Likewise, the language of an altar and going “outside the camp” joins cultic imagery with shame imagery; it is not a command to abandon vulnerable people or seek persecution.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 13:17","Hebrews 13:23-25","Hebrews 13:7","Hebrews 13:9-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_013_7219140e767426fdfee32c3f"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The final verses give the work a personal closing: prayer is requested, Timothy’s release is mentioned, leaders and saints are greeted, and people from Italy send greetings. Yet these details do not identify the author, the writer’s location, the destination, or a travel itinerary. Hebrews itself does not name its author; its description as a “word of exhortation,” together with these closing notices, accounts for why readers variously describe it as a sermon, homily, rhetorical discourse, or letter-sermon.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 13:18-25","Hebrews 13:22-25"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_013_d5cfa849519f91e440240c0d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Textual Witnesses","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Papyrus 46 is an early substantial witness to Hebrews, although parts of it are missing and its date, estimated from handwriting, remains approximate. Codex Sinaiticus also preserves Hebrews, placing it after 2 Thessalonians in its New Testament sequence.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 10:1-39","Hebrews 11:1-40","Hebrews 12:1-29","Hebrews 1:1-14","Hebrews 2:1-18","Hebrews 3:1-19","Hebrews 4:1-16","Hebrews 5:1-14","Hebrews 6:1-20","Hebrews 7:1-28","Hebrews 8:1-13","Hebrews 9:1-28"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_013_f8faff3b4f851ae573be41c9"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}